  "summary": "Emails obtained by The Maine Monitor and ACLU of Maine reveal how Flock Safety salespeople cultivated relationships with Maine police officers \u2014 including in York, Freeport, Wells, and Ogunquit \u2014 enlisting them to pitch cameras to other departments and town officials, offering free trials, conference trips, and suggesting contract-splitting to bypass town manager approval. The article situates these sales tactics against a growing backlash in Maine, where South Portland and Falmouth have removed Flock cameras, York is holding a referendum, and a state lawmaker plans a ban bill.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "I can split the cameras into two different contracts so that both items are less than $5,000, which could bypass the [Town Manager's] approval",
      "paragraph_idx": 41
    },
    {
      "quote": "They'll give you a free sample, some free cameras, and then you get kind of hooked on it. Once they get that, then it's contract time.",
      "paragraph_idx": 30
    },
    {
      "quote": "York police recently announced plans to take down Flock cameras in response to local pushback, joining other towns in Maine that have moved away from the tech.",
      "paragraph_idx": 12
    },
    {
      "quote": "South Portland pulled its cameras in June. Falmouth said it removed its Flock cameras at the summer's end.",
      "paragraph_idx": 47
    }
  ],
